; bfsodium PARALLELHASH128 : NIST SP 800_185 section 6's ParallelHash
;
; NOTE square brackets are brainfuck loops  so comments use braces for counts;
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; keccak/absorb168 is PASTED TWICE  keccak/rightenc
; twice  and keccak/squeeze168 keccak/rotstate keccak/bytepad168
; keccak/leftenc and chacha20/add32 once each;
;
; IO  in:  xof{1}  bs{2} LE  slen{2} LE  mlen{2} LE  olen{2} LE
;          sbuf{128}   (137 bytes)  then mlen bytes from the wire
;     out: olen bytes
;
;   ParallelHash128(X  B  L  S) = cSHAKE128( left_encode(B) then the
;        SHAKE128 of every B byte chunk of X in turn  256 bits each  then
;        right_encode(n) then right_encode(L)   L  "ParallelHash"  S )
;
; THIS FILE DRIVES THE SPONGE RATHER THAN PASTING ONE  and it is the first
; here that does; keccak/sponge168 takes a message it can see the end of and
; ParallelHash cannot hand it one: the outer hash's message is the run of
; digests of every chunk  which is d over B times the length of X  and at the
; standard's own B of eight that is EIGHT TIMES THE MESSAGE;
;
; SO THERE IS NO BUFFER FOR IT; each digest byte is PUSHED into the outer
; block as it comes off the inner state  and the outer block absorbs itself
; whenever it fills; The only bound on the number of chunks is mlen{2}  which
; is the bound every routine in this library already has; THERE IS NO CAP OF
; THIS ROUTINE'S OWN;
;
; A BUFFER WOULD HAVE PASSED EVERY PUBLISHED VECTOR  and that is why it was
; refused: NIST's samples are all twenty four byte messages with B equal to
; eight  so a six chunk buffer would have answered every one of them and
; refused a forty nine byte message; An implementation the tests cannot see
; through is the one thing this library will not ship;
;
; ONE OUTER LOOP AND ONE PUSH; the loop's byte comes from a staging buffer  and
; when the staging runs dry a PHASE decides what refills it: left_encode(B)
; then one chunk's digest at a time  then right_encode(n)  then
; right_encode(L)  then the padding; cSHAKE's own bytepad block is staged
; before the loop starts; The phases are six flags TESTED IN REVERSE ORDER  so
; that an arm which sets the next flag cannot fire it again in the same pass;
;
; AND THE INNER HASH IS A SPONGE WRITTEN OUT  because the one in the tree
; sends its answer to the wire and this one sends it into the outer block; It
; is cSHAKE with no name and no customization string  WHICH THE STANDARD SAYS
; IS SHAKE  so its padding byte is 31 and not 4;
;
; TWO STATES AND ONE PAIR OF ABSORB FRAMES; keccak/absorb168 wants its state
; at nought and both states cannot be there  so each is PARKED and moved down
; for its own absorb; Two hundred cells each way is eight hundred instructions
; against the permutation's two thousand four hundred and eighty four million;
;
; THE DIGEST COMES OFF THE INNER STATE IN ORDER because it is produced by a
; conveyor: each byte is placed at the staging buffer's 32th cell and the
; buffer slides down  so after 32 of them the first is at the buffer's nought;
; That works only because 32 IS A CONSTANT  which it is: the inner hash's
; output length is fixed by the standard at the rate's own capacity;
;
; SLEN IS AT MOST 148 and B AT LEAST ONE; the routine DOES NOT CHECK EITHER;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x0c7  S{200}  the state absorb squeeze and rotstate work on
;   @0x0c8:0x330  keccak/permute1600's frame  inside those pastes
;   @0x338:0x3df  the block keccak/absorb168 takes
;   @0x000:0x421  keccak/squeeze168's frame  pasted at this file's zero
;   @0x428:0x4ef  the OUTER state  parked
;   @0x4f0:0x5b7  the INNER state  parked
;   @0x5b8:0x65f  the outer block being filled
;   @0x660:0x707  the inner block being filled
;   @0x708:0x7af  the staging buffer the outer loop draws its byte from
;   @0x7b0        oc      slots left in the outer block
;   @0x7b1        ic      slots left in the inner block
;   @0x7b2        sn      bytes still staged
;   @0x7b3:0x7b4  rem{2}  message bytes still to come
;   @0x7b5:0x7b6  cb{2}   this chunk's bytes still to come
;   @0x7b7:0x7b8  n{2}    how many chunks there have been
;   @0x7b9        dc      digest bytes still to take off the inner state
;   @0x7ba        ipad    one until the inner padding byte has been placed
;   @0x7bb        ilast   one if it landed in THIS inner block
;   @0x7bc        imore   one while another inner block is wanted
;   @0x7bd        padding one while the outer block is being padded
;   @0x7be        go      one while the outer loop is running
;   @0x7bf        need    one when the staging has run dry
;   @0x7c0:0x7c1  bs{2}   the block size  kept because every chunk wants it
;   @0x7c2        pushf   one when there is a byte to push
;   @0x7c3        full    one when the outer block has filled
;   @0x7c4        xofc    the flag  as the clear reads it
;   @0x7c5:0x7ca  the six phase flags
;   @0x7cb:0x7d1  the cells the tests are made through
;   @0x7d5:0x85d  everything read  parked above the frames it arrived in
;   @0x865:0xa48  keccak/bytepad168's frame
;   @0xa4c:0xa5d  keccak/leftenc's frame
;   @0xa60:0xa71  keccak/rightenc's frame  for the chunk count
;   @0xa74:0xa85  and another for the output length
;   @0xa88:0xa9b  chacha20/add32's frame
;   @0xa9c:0xa9f  tmp{4}  hands the accumulator back to itself
;   @0xaa0        dbl     the three doublings still to do

; read the flag  the four lengths and the customization string
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
